{"text": "Lesson five, pillar two, decoding algorithms. Leave the weights alone. Spend idle tensor cores so that one sequential read of the weights can emit more than one token. The dividing line is whether the output distribution stays exactly the target models. Speculative decoding is the production technique. A cheap draft proposes a short run of candidate tokens. The expensive target scores all of them in one parallel forward pass. An acceptance rule keeps a prefix whose conditionals match the target and resamples at the 1st mismatch. Done carefully, the marginal distribution of the text is exactly the targets. The method is lossless. Lossless does not mean free. Rejected drafts are wasted work. In the memory-bound regime, that parallel check costs about the same wall clock time as a single token because the weights were going to be loaded anyway. End-to-end speedup is about the mean accepted length minus drafting and verification. The 1st drafts were separate small models. Later methods draft from the target itself. Medusa hangs extra heads on the last hidden state. Eagle drafts in feature space, where the next feature is less uncertain than the sampled token. Later Eagle versions add dynamic trees. Prompt lookup is strong when the input and the output overlap, such as code edits. A flat draft wastes the verifier if the 1st position is already wrong. Tree methods submit alternatives and take the longest valid path. Speculation helps least when the batch is already large and the target is near compute bound. Checking several tokens is no longer almost free. It is most attractive for low-batch, latency-sensitive work, chat, and code completion. Diffusion language models abandon left-to-right generation. They denoise a masked sequence over a few steps. The cost is an independence assumption inside a parallel block. Errors drift on long reasoning. These methods are lossy. Jacobi decoding updates several future positions together. For greedy decoding it can convert Verge to the same answer as stepping one token at a time. Under sampling it is lossy. Early exit leaves before the last layer when the next token is already confident. Layer skip uses that early exit as the drafter and the full model as the verifier. That combination is lossless again. Pillar two buys bandwidth. It spends compute that was sitting idle. It does not shrink memory capacity.", "language": "en", "duration": 143.4, "words": [{"text": "Lesson", "start": 0.16, "end": 0.46}, {"text": "five,", "start": 0.56, "end": 0.98}, {"text": "pillar", "start": 1.44, "end": 1.721}, {"text": "two,", "start": 1.821, "end": 2.061}, {"text": "decoding", "start": 2.461, "end": 2.981}, {"text": "algorithms.", "start": 3.101, "end": 3.741}, {"text": "Leave", "start": 4.261, "end": 4.481}, {"text": "the", "start": 4.502, "end": 4.582}, {"text": "weights", "start": 4.642, "end": 4.902}, {"text": "alone.", "start": 4.942, "end": 5.322}, {"text": "Spend", "start": 5.722, "end": 6.002}, {"text": "idle", "start": 6.182, "end": 6.362}, {"text": "tensor", "start": 6.402, "end": 6.742}, {"text": "cores", "start": 6.782, "end": 7.122}, {"text": "so", "start": 7.202, "end": 7.262}, {"text": "that", "start": 7.302, "end": 7.422}, {"text": "one", "start": 7.583, "end": 7.723}, {"text": "sequential", "start": 7.803, "end": 8.283}, {"text": "read", "start": 8.363, "end": 8.543}, {"text": "of", "start": 8.583, "end": 8.643}, {"text": "the", "start": 8.663, "end": 8.743}, {"text": "weights", "start": 8.803, "end": 9.083}, {"text": "can", "start": 9.123, "end": 9.243}, {"text": "emit", "start": 9.263, "end": 9.483}, {"text": "more", "start": 9.583, "end": 9.783}, {"text": "than", "start": 9.803, "end": 9.923}, {"text": "one", "start": 10.023, "end": 10.163}, {"text": "token.", "start": 10.203, "end": 10.584}, {"text": "The", "start": 11.244, "end": 11.324}, {"text": "dividing", "start": 11.364, "end": 11.804}, {"text": "line", "start": 11.844, "end": 12.084}, {"text": "is", "start": 12.124, "end